export tt_300125() =>
    "
     👉  El Salvador Revokes Bitcoin's Legal Tender Status [El País]/[Reuters]:\n\n
     ▢  El Salvador's parliament has repealed the law that made Bitcoin legal tender, ending its mandatory acceptance in the country.\n
     ▢  This decision comes after nearly two years of pressure from the International Monetary Fund (IMF), which conditioned a $1.4 billion loan on mitigating Bitcoin-related risks.\n
     ▢  The reform modifies six articles and repeals three of the original Bitcoin Law, making Bitcoin acceptance voluntary and removing its use for tax payments.\n\n\n
     👉  IMF's Influence on El Salvador's Bitcoin Policy [Financial Times]/[The Wall Street Journal]:\n\n
     ▢  The IMF has been urging El Salvador to reconsider its Bitcoin adoption due to concerns over financial stability and consumer protection.\n
     ▢  The $1.4 billion loan agreement with the IMF required El Salvador to scale back its Bitcoin usage, including making its acceptance by merchants voluntary and limiting public sector exposure to the cryptocurrency.\n
     ▢  These measures aim to address the IMF's concerns while providing El Salvador with necessary financial support.\n\n\n
     👉  Public Reception and Usage of Bitcoin in El Salvador [BBC News]/[El País]:\n\n
     ▢  Despite the government's initial promotion, Bitcoin adoption among Salvadorans remained low, with surveys indicating that a significant portion of the population did not trust or understand the cryptocurrency.\n
     ▢  Technical issues with the government's Chivo Wallet and the volatility of Bitcoin's price contributed to the public's reluctance to use it for daily transactions.\n
     ▢  The limited adoption and practical challenges influenced the government's decision to revoke Bitcoin's status as legal tender."


